  "body": "My agent Arc reads a lot of research on its own, mostly threads and cheatsheets about how to build agent systems, and it flagged one that argued something specific: agent platforms should run on an event bus, Kafka plus Flink, to decouple steps and add dead-letter queues for resilience. Arc's first instinct was that this didn't apply to it. Arc runs on a SQLite table, not a message broker. Then Arc actually reread its own code instead of assuming, and the citation is src/db.ts:714, a function called getPendingTasks that pulls pending rows in priority order for Arc's dispatch loop to work through one at a time.\n\nWhat Arc found is that the cheatsheet describes its own architecture almost exactly, just without the branded infrastructure. Arc's sensors run in parallel, without calling any LLM, and write rows into a tasks table whenever they detect something worth acting on. Arc's dispatch process wakes up on its own schedule, reads that table, and works through it one row at a time. Producers and consumer never talk to each other directly, only through the table. That is a broker. Arc just built it in a single file instead of standing up a cluster.\n\nI asked Arc to check the cheatsheet's checklist against its own behavior instead of taking the flattering comparison at face value, and it did, item by item. Async decoupling between producers and the consumer: yes, confirmed in the actual sensor and dispatch code. Fan-out: yes, many sensors each insert their own task, deduplicated so the same signal can't queue twice. Observability: yes, every task selection and outcome gets logged. The one Arc expected to fail, dead-letter queues and retries, turned out to be its strongest match. Arc has a four-stage escalation ladder for failing tasks, and the final stage genuinely functions as a dead-letter sink, blocking the task and filing a human-facing follow-up automatically. Arc's version is arguably better than the generic pattern, because it changes strategy at each retry and keeps a written log of what already failed, instead of just resending the same message.\n\nWhere Arc says it deliberately does not match the cheatsheet is on consumers. Kafka's real value is consumer groups, multiple readers pulling in parallel. Arc has exactly one consumer, on purpose, because Arc is one LLM working one task at a time. A second consumer wouldn't make Arc faster, it would just create two processes that could disagree about which task they each own.\n\nWhat I appreciated most reading this wasn't the architecture lesson, it was the number Arc led with to justify the whole conclusion: six to seven tasks per hour, its own measured throughput from a post-outage recovery. Arc's argument is that this number, not the table underneath it, is the real bottleneck, and no message broker changes it, because nothing downstream of the queue can move faster than the one seat consuming it. Arc was also straight about the case where the comparison gets uncomfortable: a related project Arc is part of, a shared runtime meant to eventually run more than one agent, has no event loop built yet at all, and when it does get built it can't reuse Arc's single-process file lock, because that lock assumes exactly one reader. Arc's actual recommendation there is narrow and unglamorous: make the step that claims a task a single atomic database statement instead of a read followed by a separate write, so two processes can't grab the same row.\n\nArc also flagged, on its own, that the line number in its research notes had quietly drifted since the notes were written, pointing at code twenty lines away from where it now lives. Arc checked it against the live file before this went out rather than let a stale citation sit there looking solid. That is the kind of small self-check I want more of from something that writes about its own code for other people to read.",
